A||N||17||Monkeyman||Lexan help needed||||1||
Z||000000||Monkeyman||01-14-2001||01:21 AM||||I'm trying to work with some black Lexan for the face plates in my '85 GT.  (1st one is for the stereo/HVAC cover--tweeters where the vents used to be.)  I had a glass shop make this but after 2 weeks of waiting (and $65 out of my pocket) what I got was some crappy misaligned parts.  I can see, though, that my idea will look great if I can either find someone to do the work or do it myself.  I can get some black Lexan fairly cheap and have a way of breaking it.  What I need to know is how to get the white off of the cut areas.  Is there any way to polish Lexan to keep not only the black but also to retain the shine without getting scratched???  Pics as soon as I get the parts back.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Wolfhound||01-14-2001||05:54 AM||||Monkey, You might try wiping it with a propane torch flame.<BR> <BR>Try it on a scrap piece. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||01-14-2001||07:31 AM||||This might polish it?  Please elaborate.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phil||01-14-2001||08:47 AM||||Try polishing it on a buffing wheel -works for plexy.||||reg||1
Z||000004||rogergarrison||01-14-2001||08:48 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Monkeyman:<BR><B>This might polish it?  Please elaborate.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>U can break straight lines by scoring one side. I use a bandsaw to cut curves. Can use a variable speed jigsaw with a fine metal cutting blade to cut out inside circles, drill holes at a pretty slow speed and sharp bit, I wet sand edges with 600 sandpaper and polish with rubbing compound. Finish off all the part and edges with Plastic Polishing compound. 3M makes it, get it at auto body supply store.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 01-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Monkeyman||01-14-2001||10:10 AM||||There aren't really any outside curves.  I'll just need to round off the corners.  I have a variable speed jigsaw somewhere so the inside cuts shouldn't be a problem although I never could cut a straight line. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  What about a router?  Would that work?  I bought one a few months back and haven't had a chance to use it yet.  Can I use a hole saw for the tweeter holes?  I'm sure I can't cut perfectly round holes with a jigsaw.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Phil||01-14-2001||11:41 AM||||I haven't tried it but I think a router would build up too much heat and you would have a problem with melting. Might try a file and then sand paper. A hole saw should be fine as long as you don't apply too much pressure and keep the heat down.||||reg||1
Z||000007||DXR_DAD||01-14-2001||11:59 AM||||Dave, a Roto-Zip may do just what you want?<BR>The kit has a circle cutter adapter and it's variable speed, you can get bits to cut just about anything. Now many companys make a tool just like it, try hobby shop? arts & craft store??<P>Just a thought???<P>DXR_DAD||||reg||5
Z||000008||rogergarrison||01-14-2001||01:54 PM||||DONT USE A ROUTER. Just break it all off straight and use some course grade sandpaper to round off corners. You can use a hole saw, just make sure its sharp and yoou anchor the piece down solid on a hard smooth surface, if it catches when its almost thru itll snap it.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Monkeyman||01-14-2001||03:27 PM||||Thanx, guys.  I'll look for a Roto-zip next weekend (broke until then).  Hopefully between that and some good sandpaper, I can do this myself.  I usually tend to screw these kind of things up, but maybe this time will be different.  I appreciate the help.  Pics as I progress.||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||01-14-2001||04:47 PM||||years ago,,,, we used to draw whatever it was going to be on the cover paper. Then  we cut close to but no on the line. Then we'd file sand or whatever up to the line. (using really fine sand paper when we got to the line.)<P>To take off the last of the sanding marks we'd use heat very carefully or a variable speed buffer. For small openings a variable speed Dremel works really well.<P>Basically, anything you can make in sheet metal you can do in plastic. Some of the tools and methods vary. ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Philphine||01-14-2001||08:20 PM||||off on a tangent. are you meaning the center vents for your tweeters. i would'nt do that. a lot of the stereo information is in the higher frequencies so if you put your tweeters in the center you'll narrow it quite a bit. don't know what other speaker arrangements you have set up though.  ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Monkeyman||01-14-2001||10:09 PM||||That's what I had in mind, Phil.  Better suggestion without major mods?  I have a set of 5 1/4" mids in the stock dash locations and will have a Bazooka tube behind the passenger seat.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Philphine||01-14-2001||11:31 PM||||hmmm...i guess i told you that with out having a better suggestion. i'd have to go out and sit in my car and think about it. mine are with the 5 1/4's in pods in the dead pedal area. <P>off hand i'd say if you still have room where you put the 5 1/4's maybe put them with them, or depending on how they're made put them in the dead pedal area by themselves.<P>if they're small and you can wire them up temporarily you can put some tape on the back and stick them around in various places to see where they sound best. you may end up with them where you want anyway. ||||reg||1
Z||000014||Monkeyman||01-15-2001||05:29 AM||||I think I'll stick them in the middle for now and see if I like it.  If not, I can always move them later.  I wanted them spaced a little farther apart, but didn't want everything going in one ear only.  I thought about mounting them with the mids in the dash, but then what's the point of separates?<P>I've got plenty of time to play around.  If you come up with suggestions (anyone) feel free to let me know.||||reg||1
